  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Haiti's appearance in 2026 is one of the tournament's most profound stories — a nation enduring political collapse, gang violence, and humanitarian crisis sending a football team to the world's biggest stage. Every match they play will carry impossible weight.",
    "footballIdentity": "Football is Haiti's escape and its pride. In one of the Western Hemisphere's poorest countries, battered by earthquakes, hurricanes, political assassination, and gang warfare that controls large portions of the capital, football provides something nothing else can: collective hope. The 1974 World Cup appearance — their first and only until now — remains a national landmark. Half a century later, they're back.",
    "theMoment": "Haiti qualified for the 2026 World Cup while the country was in effective collapse. Prime Minister Ariel Henry resigned in 2024 amid gang warfare that made Port-au-Prince one of the most dangerous cities on earth. A transitional council has struggled to establish order. The football team — many of whom play in leagues across the Americas and Europe — represents a Haiti that could be. Their presence in Group C is simultaneously absurd and deeply moving.",
    "momentType": "historical"
  },
